# Core Concepts and Glossary

The project aims to build a unified commerce platform for small-to-medium e-commerce merchants. This single system will handle products, orders, customers, and inventory, serving as a single source of truth. Currently, these businesses rely on fragmented tools like spreadsheets, Shopify, and email, leading to manual processes and data discrepancies.

The initial version (v1) of the platform will include product catalog management, comprehensive order lifecycle handling, customer account management, and inventory tracking capabilities across multiple warehouses.

A Customer is defined as a registered user of the platform who has the ability to browse products and place orders.

A Product represents an item available for sale, characterized by its price, a descriptive overview, and its current stock level.

An Order signifies a completed transaction, linking a specific customer to one or more products they have purchased.

Inventory refers to the recorded stock counts for each product, meticulously tracked on a per-warehouse basis.

A Line Item is a distinct entry within an order, detailing a single product along with its purchased quantity and the unit price at the time of sale.

A Merchant is the business entity that operates the e-commerce storefront and utilizes this unified commerce platform.

Key roles involved in the system include Customers who browse and purchase, Merchant Admins who manage products and review orders, Operations staff handling picking, packing, and shipping, and Platform Engineering responsible for system development and maintenance.

The business process involves customers placing orders, which contain line items referencing specific products. Each product's inventory is tracked by warehouse. Order fulfillment triggers inventory decrementation and initiates the shipment process.

Currently, most merchants manage their e-commerce operations using a fragmented toolset. This typically involves Shopify for storefront management, Google Sheets for inventory tracking, and email for coordinating order fulfillment. This multi-system approach often leads to data existing in disparate locations and frequently falling out of synchronization.

A key indicator of project success is achieving a report from 95% of pilot merchants that indicates faster order processing times after adopting the new platform.

Another critical success criterion is the elimination of inventory discrepancies, aiming for zero discrepancies after 30 days of parallel operation with the new system.

