Prior to beta release, the system will undergo a load test simulating 500 concurrent users. This validation step aims to assess performance and stability under expected load conditions.

A 2-week parallel run will be conducted to compare the outputs of the new system against the old system. This validation method ensures functional equivalence and identifies discrepancies.

An automated regression suite will be developed to cover every acceptance criterion. This suite will be used to ensure that new changes do not introduce regressions in existing functionality.

The delivery plan is structured into several phases. Weeks 1-3 will focus on core product CRUD functionality and customer registration, establishing foundational capabilities.

Weeks 4-6 of the delivery plan are dedicated to implementing order placement and comprehensive inventory management features. This phase expands the system's transactional capabilities.

Payment integration and the development of the admin dashboard are scheduled for Weeks 7-9. This phase finalizes key operational and management components of the product.

The go-to-market strategy begins in Week 10 with a beta launch involving 10 pilot merchants. This initial phase will gather real-world feedback and validate core functionality.

Following the initial beta, the program will expand to include 50 merchants in Week 12. General availability (GA) is targeted for Week 16, marking the broad release of the product.

Four weeks after General Availability, the old system will be officially sunsetted. This final step completes the migration and fully transitions operations to the new platform.

