# User Journeys and Workflows

The typical customer journey begins with browsing the product catalog and adding items to a cart. Upon checkout, the system validates stock availability and calculates the total. After successful payment processing, the order is confirmed, triggering warehouse operations for picking, packing, and shipping, which in turn decrements inventory. Finally, the customer receives a shipping confirmation with tracking details.

> source: sources/BUSINESS_DOMAIN_KNOWLEDGE.md | weight: 1.0 | hash: ae5c880e

Customers can browse and search the product catalog, add items to their cart, place orders, view their order history, and track shipments. Administrators are able to add, update, and remove products, as well as view and manage all orders within the system.

Customers must be able to view a paginated list of products, with a default of 20 items displayed per page. Upon placing an order, customers should receive an order confirmation within 3 seconds.

Administrators are required to be able to Create, Read, Update, and Delete (CRUD) products through the dedicated admin API.

> source: sources/PRODUCT_REQUIREMENTS.md | weight: 0.8 | hash: b94185cd

The new system replaces several existing workflows. Per-merchant spreadsheets for data management will be replaced by a centralized PostgreSQL database. The previous email-based order flow will transition to a REST API with automated state transitions, and nightly batch inventory reconciliation will be upgraded to a real-time synchronization process.

The migration strategy involves three phases. In Phase 1, the new API will be deployed alongside existing tools, allowing merchants to opt in. Phase 2 focuses on importing historical data for the last two years per merchant, using CSV files. Finally, in Phase 3, old workflows will be sunsetted once each merchant confirms parity and functionality with the new system.

