# Constraints and Hard Rules

Features such as marketplace/multi-vendor support, subscription billing functionality, and analytics dashboards are explicitly out of scope for v1. These advanced capabilities are planned for consideration in subsequent versions (v2) of the platform.

A critical performance requirement for the platform is to maintain customer-facing API uptime above 99.9% throughout the first quarter of operation.

> source: sources/BUSINESS_DOMAIN_KNOWLEDGE.md | weight: 1.0 | hash: ae5c880e

All API endpoints must enforce JWT authentication for security. This is a mandatory requirement for accessing any part of the system's programmatic interface.

Order totals must be computed exclusively on the server-side, with any client-provided totals being disregarded. This ensures accuracy and prevents client-side manipulation of order values.

Inventory levels are strictly prohibited from going negative. Any order that would result in an understocked inventory position must be rejected by the system to maintain stock accuracy.

The product search functionality must return results within 200 milliseconds for catalogs containing up to 100,000 items. This is a critical performance acceptance criterion for the system.

The system must achieve and maintain PCI-DSS compliance for all payment handling operations. This ensures that sensitive payment card data is processed and stored securely according to industry standards.

All sensitive user data must be encrypted both at rest using AES-256 encryption and in transit using TLS 1.3. This protocol ensures confidentiality and integrity of data across its lifecycle.

Public API endpoints will enforce a rate limit of 100 requests per minute per IP address. This measure helps protect the system from abuse and ensures service availability.

Product prices must adhere to a strict format: they must be positive decimal numbers and always have exactly two decimal places. This ensures accurate financial calculations.

The order status will follow a strict state machine progression. Orders will transition from 'pending' to 'confirmed', then to 'shipped', and finally to 'delivered'. This ensures consistent order lifecycle management.

The system will not perform any hard deletes for core entities such as customers, orders, or products. Instead, these entities will only be soft-deleted, allowing for potential recovery and maintaining historical integrity.

A critical stop condition for the beta rollout is an order error rate exceeding 1%. If this threshold is breached, the rollout will be halted to address underlying issues.

If the 95th percentile latency on any API endpoint exceeds 5 seconds, a performance review will be triggered. This indicates a potential bottleneck requiring investigation and optimization.

In the event of a critical failure, the system mandates an immediate revert to the legacy workflow. This recovery action must be completed within 2 hours to minimize disruption.

