# Risks and Open Questions

Existing operational challenges include significant inventory drift between systems, causing merchants to regularly oversell products. Order processing is labor-intensive, often involving manual copy-pasting between emails and spreadsheets. Additionally, there is no unified view of customer data, requiring support staff to check multiple tools for inquiries, and reporting necessitates weekly manual data aggregation across various platforms.

> source: sources/BUSINESS_DOMAIN_KNOWLEDGE.md | weight: 1.0 | hash: ae5c880e

The specific Shopify webhook payload format required for inventory synchronization is an open question that needs a dedicated technical spike. This will inform how external inventory updates are processed.

It is currently unknown whether existing merchants utilize consistent CSV formats for product import. This inconsistency could pose challenges for data migration and onboarding new merchants.

There is no historical traffic baseline available for new merchants, which means their peak usage patterns are currently unknown. This presents a risk for capacity planning and potential performance issues during high load.

In the event of a Stripe outage, orders will be queued and retried automatically. During this period, customers will see their order status as "processing" to manage expectations.

A database failover event is expected to result in a 10–30 second read-only window, particularly during critical periods like promotions. This temporary state will affect transactional operations.

Should an S3 outage occur, product pages will render without images. This is considered a graceful degradation, meaning the system will avoid a hard failure and remain partially functional.

> source: sources/PRODUCT_REQUIREMENTS.md | weight: 0.8 | hash: b94185cd

Several tricky scenarios have been identified that require careful consideration. These include managing inventory race conditions, such as when two customers simultaneously attempt to order the last available unit. Another complexity arises with products that have variants like size or color, but share a common base SKU. Additionally, the system must handle split shipments effectively when ordered items originate from different warehouses.

