# Infrastructure and Integration

The platform's backend is built with Node.js/Express, utilizing PostgreSQL as the primary database and Redis for caching and rate limiting. Product images are stored in AWS S3, payments are processed through Stripe, and transactional emails are handled by SendGrid. The frontend is a React single-page application (SPA) that interacts with the backend via REST APIs.

Shopify will remain the storefront for merchants currently using it, with integration to the new platform occurring via webhooks. Stripe will also continue to be used as the payment processor, maintaining an existing external dependency.

The system relies on several key external dependencies. PostgreSQL 15 or higher serves as the primary data store, while Redis is used for managing sessions, rate limiting, and caching. The Stripe API handles all payment processing, and AWS S3 is utilized for storing product images. SendGrid is integrated for sending transactional emails.

The infrastructure must support 500 concurrent users while maintaining sub-second response times. Database operations require point-in-time recovery capabilities with a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of 5 minutes. Deployments will be Docker-based, enabling zero-downtime rolling updates for continuous service availability.

